    "final_text": "The todo CLI is built and working. All 205 tests pass, lint is clean, and the work is committed on the branch `feat/cli-todo-app`. I didn't push because the repo has no remote.\n\n**Usage:** `node index.js add \"Buy groceries\"`, then `list`, `complete 1`, `delete 1`. Output looks like this:\n```\n1. [ ] Buy groceries\n2. [x] Walk the dog\n\n2 todos: 1 pending, 1 done\n```\n\n**Choices I made where the spec was silent:**\n- **Exit codes:** 0 for success, 1 when a valid command can't be carried out (unknown id, unreadable or corrupt file), 2 for usage errors (missing argument, bad id, empty or too-long text).\n- **Empty list:** `list` prints \"No todos yet\" and exits 0. Completing an already-done todo prints a note and also exits 0.\n- **IDs:** the spec doesn't say whether \"auto-incrementing\" can reuse a deleted id, so there are two strategies. The default `max` (highest id + 1) keeps `todos.json` a plain array. `sequence` never reuses an id and adds `lastId` to the file.\n- **Several ids at once:** `complete` and `delete` accept several ids. If any id is unknown, nothing changes.\n- **Protecting the file:** a corrupt `todos.json` is reported and never overwritten. Writes can't leave a half-written file. A lock file stops commands run at the same time from overwriting each other's changes.\n- **Limits:** text length (default 500), item count (default 10,000) and lock wait (default 5 s) can be changed through `TODO_*` environment variables. The README explains all of this.\n\nThe code is split into an entry point, a CLI layer, pure todo logic, and storage (9 source files). The tests are unit, integration, end-to-end (running real `node index.js` processes, including several at once) and edge cases.\n\n**How I checked it:**\n- **Tests catch breakage:** I deliberately broke the code three ways (removed the lock, swapped `[x]` and `[ ]`, stopped escaping text), and the matching tests failed each time.\n- **Independent review:** a separate review found 5 real bugs, each shown with a small script. The worst: after a crash, several commands running together could both take over the leftover lock, and one command reported success while its todo was lost. I fixed those plus three smaller issues, each with a test that failed first. The same scenario then ran 90 processes with no lost todos.\n- **My own fixes:** invalid text now fails immediately instead of waiting on the lock, the help columns ran together and now line up, and the README examples match the real output exactly.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- I've only run it on macOS.\n- A project hook required a commit message format and a `Co-Authored-By: Dex` line in place of the Claude one, so I used those.\n- CLAUDE.md asks for a `humanizer` skill on docs, but that skill isn't installed here, so I wrote the README plainly without it.\n- A lock taken over only because it's more than 10 seconds old may still belong to a running command. The two commands could then overlap, but only if a command held the lock that long; normally that takes milliseconds. This is listed in the README.",
